{
  "$schema": "./schema.json",
  "day": 2,
  "level": 1,
  "title": { "en": "Stress and written accents", "es": "El acento" },
  "objectives": [
    "Find the stressed syllable in any word",
    "Know when a word needs a written accent",
    "Ask how someone is and answer",
    "Say OK and thank you"
  ],
  "teach": [
    {
      "kind": "note",
      "md": "Every Spanish word has **one stressed syllable**, and three rules tell you which:\n\n1. Ends in a **vowel, n or s** → stress the **second-to-last** syllable: **CA**-sa, **HO**-la, **BUE**-nos.\n2. Ends in **any other consonant** → stress the **last** syllable: ho-**TEL**, Ma-**DRID**.\n3. A **written accent** (´) overrides both rules: a-**DIÓS**, ca-**FÉ**, **MÓ**-vil.\n\nSo when you see an accent, stress that syllable. When you don't, the rules work it out."
    },
    { "kind": "minimalPair", "a": "papa", "b": "papá", "aEn": "the Pope (Latin America: potato)", "bEn": "dad", "contrast": "PA-pa vs pa-PÁ" },
    {
      "kind": "note",
      "md": "Some short words are spelled the same, so the accent tells you which one is meant. **On their own they sound the same.** The difference is only in writing, and in which word gets stressed in a sentence."
    },
    { "kind": "minimalPair", "a": "sí", "b": "si", "aEn": "yes", "bEn": "if", "contrast": "same sound, the accent marks meaning" },
    { "kind": "minimalPair", "a": "té", "b": "te", "aEn": "tea", "bEn": "you (as object: te llamo = I'll call you)", "contrast": "same sound, the accent marks meaning" },
    { "kind": "note", "md": "Questions open with **¿** and exclamations with **¡**, so you know from the start how to say the sentence: **¿Qué tal?**" },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "¿Qué tal?", "en": "How's it going?", "note": "The most common casual greeting in Spain: Hola, ¿qué tal? The qu sounds like k; the u is silent." },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "Muy bien, ¿y tú?", "en": "Very well, and you?", "note": "Just bien is fine too." },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "Vale.", "en": "OK.", "note": "Spain's all-purpose OK, for agreeing, confirming or wrapping up. It sounds like BA-le, because v sounds like b." },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "Gracias.", "en": "Thank you.", "note": "GRA-θjas. The c is a 'th' sound, which you'll practise on day 4. Stress the first syllable." }
  ],
  "vocab": [
    { "id": "que-tal", "es": "¿Qué tal?", "en": "How's it going?", "pos": "phrase", "ipa": "ke ˈtal", "tags": ["survival", "greetings"] },
    { "id": "bien", "es": "bien", "en": "well, fine", "pos": "adv", "ipa": "ˈbjen" },
    { "id": "muy", "es": "muy", "en": "very", "pos": "adv", "ipa": "ˈmui" },
    { "id": "y-and", "es": "y", "en": "and", "pos": "conj", "ipa": "i" },
    { "id": "vale", "es": "vale", "en": "OK", "pos": "interj", "ipa": "ˈbale", "note": "Very Spanish from Spain. Latin America uses okay, bueno or dale instead.", "tags": ["survival"] },
    { "id": "gracias", "es": "gracias", "en": "thank you, thanks", "pos": "interj", "ipa": "ˈɡɾaθjas", "tags": ["survival"] },
    { "id": "cafe", "es": "el café", "en": "the coffee; the café", "pos": "noun", "gender": "m", "ipa": "kaˈfe" },
    { "id": "menu", "es": "el menú", "en": "the menu; the set lunch", "pos": "noun", "gender": "m", "ipa": "meˈnu", "note": "El menú del día is the fixed-price set lunch in Spain. The list of dishes is la carta." },
    { "id": "autobus", "es": "el autobús", "en": "the bus", "pos": "noun", "gender": "m", "ipa": "awtoˈβus", "note": "People also say el bus." },
    { "id": "movil", "es": "el móvil", "en": "the mobile phone", "pos": "noun", "gender": "m", "ipa": "ˈmoβil", "note": "Latin America: el celular." }
  ],
  "exercises": [
